before:
Here is a complete example.
```javascript
const typed = "  River Park  ";
const clean = typed.trim();
console.log(clean);
console.log(typed.length > clean.length);
```
It prints River Park, then true. The middle space stays. length counts the string's UTF-16 code units; it is useful for this empty-text check but does not always count visible letters.

A fallback is the value you use when the usual input is missing.
```javascript
function label(text) {
  const clean = text.trim();
  if (clean === "") return "Unnamed";
  return "Name: " + clean;
}
console.log(label("  Oak  "));
console.log(label("   "));
```
This prints Name: Oak, then Unnamed. return ends this function call and gives a value back to its caller.

after:
Keep the space between the two words:
```javascript
const typed = "  River Park  ";
const clean = typed.trim();
console.log(clean);
console.log(typed.length > clean.length);
```
Output:
```text
River Park
true
```
`length` counts UTF-16 units, the pieces JavaScript uses to store text. A visible letter or symbol can use more than one unit.

A fallback is the answer used when the usual input is missing:
```javascript
function label(text) {
  const clean = text.trim();
  if (clean === "") return "Unnamed";
  return "Name: " + clean;
}
console.log(label("  Oak  "));
console.log(label("   "));
```
Output:
```text
Name: Oak
Unnamed
```
`return` gives back the chosen label and ends the call.
